    "reasoning": "The earlier mathematical conclusions remain supported. Fifteen questions are confirmed by their own captured worked solutions. q-47077 contains a source error: both [-2,0] and [-2,1] permit IVT. On [-2,1], the restricted function equals x+1 everywhere and is continuous, including at the right endpoint. MA's selected answer [-2,0] is valid, so it remains the returned selection, but MA's assertion that it is the only valid option is false. q-12706: two integrations by parts give x^3((ln x)^2/3-2 ln x/9+2/27)+C, equivalent to option 0. q-8479: x=5\\pi/24+n\\pi/2; the first two roots give 85\\pi^2/576. q-82373: x=45+90n in degrees; 45 times 135 is 6075. q-77809: the cosecant range excludes values with absolute value below 1, so I and III have no solutions. q-82529: the composite map is (x,y) to (x,-y-x/2), matching option 0. q-82525: the composite map is (x,y) to (y,-x-y/2), matching option 4, including its transformed lettering. q-12257: diag(1/6,1) times the rotation matrix gives option 1. q-48430: IVT requires continuity and a target between the endpoint values inclusively, giving I, II. q-48334: the endpoint values are 6 and 2. q-105684: f(1)=2 and f(4)=66; k=1 is outside the guaranteed interval and below all function values on [1,4]. q-47101: g(2)=0 and g(4)=-12; g is nonpositive on [2,4], excluding k=1. q-78542: crossings are forced in (-1,1) and (1,2), giving a minimum of two. q-105701: crossings are forced in (2,3), (3,4), and (4,5), giving a minimum of three. Piecewise linear interpolation attains both minimum counts. q-118090 and q-118135: the standard exponential limits give e^{xy} and e^{xy^2} for fixed real x,y. No captured grade is supplied; all MA evidence below comes from worked solutions.",
